{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>Every firstborn — you shall consecrate.</b> And in another place (Leviticus 27) it says ״לֹא יַקְדִּישׁ אִישׁ אֹתוֹ״. How so? He does not consecrate it for a different offering; and here it teaches that it is a commandment to say: Behold, you are consecrated for firstbornhood. Another interpretation — it is impossible to say ״תַּקְדִּישׁ,״ for ״לֹא יַקְדִּישׁ״ has already been said, and it is impossible to say ״לֹא יַקְדִּישׁ,״ for ״תַּקְדִּישׁ״ has already been said. How so? You consecrate it as a consecrated substitute and give to the temple treasury the value of the benefit of enjoyment in it (Arakhin 29)." "<b>You shall not work with your firstborn ox, and you shall not shear, etc.</b> Our Rabbis also learned the exchange — that it is forbidden; only, Scripture spoke of what is common (Bekhorot 25)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:47:16.710701236Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}